Yes. Rate limiting is only one small part of the solution.

For a breaking-news site, the important architectural idea is:

50,000 requests/sec should NOT mean 50,000 requests/sec hitting Django + PostgreSQL.

You want the majority of those requests to be served before they reach your Django application.

A good architecture looks like this:

                    INTERNET
                       │
                       ▼
              ┌─────────────────┐
              │ CDN / Edge Cache│
              │ Cloudflare etc. │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       │
              90-99% cache HIT
                       │
              ┌────────▼────────┐
              │ Load Balancer   │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       │
             ┌─────────┴─────────┐
             ▼                   ▼
       Django ASGI #1       Django ASGI #2
             │                   │
             └─────────┬─────────┘
                       │
              ┌────────▼────────┐
              │ Redis Cache      │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       │
                       ▼
                PostgreSQL
                       │
                       ▼
                 Background
                  Workers


The key is moving work outward.

1. First principle: don't make Django handle every request

Suppose your breaking-news article gets:

50,000 requests/sec


If every request does:

article = Article.objects.get(slug=slug)

comments = Comment.objects.filter(article=article)

related = Article.objects.filter(...)

return render(...)


you're in trouble.

Even if you have many Django servers, you're still creating enormous pressure on:

PostgreSQL
Redis
Django workers
Python
network
template rendering

Instead, make the request path progressively cheaper:

Request
  ↓
CDN cache
  ↓ cache miss
Reverse proxy cache
  ↓ cache miss
Redis
  ↓ cache miss
Django
  ↓
Database


Ideally:

50,000 req/s
     ↓
49,000+ served by CDN
     ↓
1,000 reach infrastructure
     ↓
100 reach Django
     ↓
10 reach PostgreSQL


The exact percentages aren't universal, but that's the design objective.

Django itself provides site-level and per-view caching, and Redis is supported as a cache backend. 
