2. Cache the actual news pages

This is probably the single most important code-level optimization for your use case.

News websites have an unusual characteristic:

Millions of people may request exactly the same URL.

For example:

/news/nepal-earthquake-2026


10,000 people might request the exact same article in a few seconds.

There is absolutely no reason for Django to regenerate the page 10,000 times.

You want:

GET /news/nepal-earthquake-2026

        ↓

CDN
   │
   ├── HIT → return HTML
   │
   └── MISS
          ↓
       Django
          ↓
       generate
          ↓
       cache


Then subsequent requests don't reach Django.

For example:

from django.views.decorators.cache import cache_page


@cache_page(10)
def article(request, slug):
    article = (
        Article.objects
        .select_related("author")
        .get(slug=slug)
    )

    return render(
        request,
        "news/article.html",
        {"article": article},
    )


Even a 5–10 second cache can dramatically reduce load during a breaking-news spike.

And here's the nice part:

You don't need the page to be cached for 5 minutes.

For breaking news:

TTL = 5 seconds


can already be extremely powerful.

3. Use CDN caching aggressively

For a news website, I would put a CDN in front of basically everything that can be cached:

                         CDN
                          │
             ┌────────────┼────────────┐
             │            │            │
          HTML          Images       JS/CSS
             │
             ▼
          Django


Static assets should essentially never hit Django.

For example:

/static/app.js
/static/style.css
/media/article-image.webp
/media/video-thumbnail.webp


should go:

Browser → CDN → object storage


rather than:

Browser → Django → filesystem


And article HTML can also be cached at the CDN.

For example:

Cache-Control: public, s-maxage=10, stale-while-revalidate=30


This gives you something extremely useful for breaking news:

10 sec fresh
30 sec stale-but-servable


So when the article changes, the CDN doesn't suddenly send thousands of simultaneous requests to Django.

4. Use stale-while-revalidate

This is particularly valuable for your use case.

Imagine:

50,000 users


all request the same article at:

12:00:00


The cache expires.

A naïve system might do:

50,000 requests
      ↓
50,000 cache misses
      ↓
50,000 Django requests
      ↓
50,000 DB queries


Bad.

Instead:

50,000 requests
      ↓
CDN
      ↓
serve stale version
      │
      └── one background refresh


Conceptually:

                 ┌───────────────┐
50k requests →   │ stale article │
                 └───────────────┘
                         │
                         │ 1 refresh
                         ▼
                      Django


This prevents the thundering herd problem.

5. Prevent cache stampedes inside Django

Even Redis caching can have this problem.

Bad:

article = cache.get(key)

if article is None:
    article = Article.objects.get(slug=slug)
    cache.set(key, article, 30)


If the cache expires and 5,000 Django requests arrive simultaneously:

5,000 cache misses
       ↓
5,000 DB queries


Instead, use a locking/single-flight strategy.

Conceptually:

value = cache.get(key)

if value is not None:
    return value

with distributed_lock(key):
    value = cache.get(key)

    if value is not None:
        return value

    value = expensive_database_operation()

    cache.set(key, value, 30)

return value


Now:

5,000 requests
      ↓
Redis miss
      ↓
1 request gets lock
      ↓
1 DB query
      ↓
Redis populated
      ↓
4,999 requests use cache


This is much more important than simply adding rate limiting.

6. Don't cache only the entire page

You can use several layers.

For example:

ARTICLE PAGE
│
├── Article body             → cache 10 sec
├── Author                   → cache 5 min
├── Related articles         → cache 60 sec
├── Trending articles        → cache 5 sec
├── Comments count           → cache 5 sec
└── User-specific data       → don't cache publicly


That allows you to compose a page without repeatedly querying PostgreSQL.

7. Make your database almost irrelevant to normal reads

For a news site, most requests are reads.

So your architecture should be:

                    READ
                     │
                     ▼
                  CDN
                     │
                cache miss
                     ▼
                  Redis
                     │
                cache miss
                     ▼
                  Django
                     │
                cache miss
                     ▼
                PostgreSQL


PostgreSQL should be the source of truth, not the thing serving every visitor.

8. Database query optimization still matters

Caching doesn't mean you can write terrible queries.

For example:

articles = Article.objects.all()


and then:

for article in articles:
    print(article.author.name)


can cause N+1 queries.

Use:

articles = (
    Article.objects
    .select_related("author")
    .prefetch_related("categories")
)


And only retrieve fields you actually need:

Article.objects.values(
    "id",
    "slug",
    "title",
    "published_at",
)


Also make sure frequently queried fields have indexes:

class Article(models.Model):
    slug = models.SlugField(unique=True)
    published_at = models.DateTimeField(db_index=True)


For example:

GET /news/some-story


should ideally become:

SELECT ...
FROM article
WHERE slug = 'some-story'
LIMIT 1;


with an index.

9. Separate "publishing" from "reading"

This is a very important architectural decision for a breaking-news site.

Don't make publishing an article immediately trigger expensive work synchronously.

Bad:

Editor publishes article
        ↓
Django request
        ↓
update DB
        ↓
generate homepage
generate related articles
generate recommendations
generate social metadata
resize images
send notifications
update trending
invalidate 50 caches
        ↓
response


Instead:

Editor publishes
       ↓
PostgreSQL
       ↓
transaction committed
       ↓
queue
       ├── invalidate cache
       ├── generate thumbnails
       ├── update search index
       ├── update trending
       ├── send notifications
       └── update recommendations


Use something like:

Celery
+
Redis/RabbitMQ


or another queue system.

The web request should do the minimum necessary work.

10. Think about your homepage differently

The homepage is usually one of the most expensive endpoints.

Don't do this on every request:

def homepage(request):

    latest = Article.objects.filter(...)

    trending = Article.objects.filter(...)

    popular = Article.objects.filter(...)

    recommended = ...

    return render(...)


Instead, periodically build the homepage data.

For example:

Database
    ↓
background worker
    ↓
generate homepage
    ↓
Redis
    ↓
CDN


You can have:

homepage:v1
homepage:v2
homepage:v3


or simply:

homepage


in Redis.

Then Django does:

homepage = cache.get("homepage")

return HttpResponse(homepage)


Very cheap.

11. Pre-render important pages

For a news website, I'd seriously consider static HTML generation for very popular articles.

For example:

Breaking news happens
        ↓
Django creates article
        ↓
render HTML
        ↓
push/cache HTML at CDN


Then:

50,000 users
       ↓
CDN
       ↓
HTML


Django doesn't participate in those requests.

This is essentially turning:

dynamic website


into:

mostly static website


for the highest-traffic content.

That is an extremely powerful pattern for news.

12. Don't make every endpoint dynamic

You might have:

/news/foo                 → cache heavily
/news/bar                 → cache heavily
/                         → cache heavily
/category/politics        → cache
/category/sports          → cache

/api/search               → dynamic
/api/login                → dynamic
/api/comment              → dynamic
/api/profile              → dynamic
/api/notifications        → dynamic


Your architecture can distinguish between:

public/cacheable


and:

private/dynamic


That's much better than treating every request equally.

13. Rate limiting is for protection, not scaling

Rate limiting is still useful.

But its purpose should be:

protect infrastructure


not:

handle 50k legitimate users


For example:

Anonymous article:
    100 requests/sec/IP → probably unnecessary to block aggressively

Login:
    5 requests/minute/IP

Search:
    20 requests/minute/IP

Comment:
    30 requests/minute/user

Expensive API:
    10 requests/sec/user


You don't want:

50,000 legitimate users
       ↓
rate limiter
       ↓
429 Too Many Requests


Instead:

50,000 legitimate GET requests
       ↓
CDN
       ↓
served normally


while abusive traffic gets blocked.

14. Use different rate limits for different endpoints

This is important.

Don't have:

100 requests/minute


globally.

Instead:

/news/*
    CDN/cache

/search
    30/minute/user

/login
    5/minute/IP

/comments
    20/minute/user

/admin/*
    strict

/api/trending
    cache heavily


The expensive operations need much stronger protection.

15. Django ASGI can help—but don't think async magically gives you 50k RPS

If you're on modern Django, ASGI is worth considering.

Django supports asynchronous views and async APIs, and ASGI is particularly useful for high-concurrency I/O workloads. 
