For example:

async def article(request, slug):
    article = await Article.objects.aget(slug=slug)

    return JsonResponse(...)


But don't fall into this trap:

"I'll convert everything to async and Django will handle 50k RPS."

No.

Async helps when you're waiting on I/O:

HTTP API
Redis
database
network
streaming
SSE


It doesn't eliminate:

database bottlenecks
CPU
serialization
template rendering
network bandwidth
cache misses


And mixing synchronous middleware/code into an async stack can reduce the benefit. Django's documentation specifically calls this out. 
