27. What I'd prioritize

If you're building this from scratch, I'd prioritize these in this order:

Priority	Technique	Importance
🔥 1	CDN/edge caching	Extremely high
🔥 2	Full-page HTML caching	Extremely high
🔥 3	Redis application cache	Extremely high
🔥 4	Prevent cache stampede	Extremely high
🔥 5	Database query/index optimization	Very high
🔥 6	Background jobs	Very high
🔥 7	Async/ASGI where appropriate	High
8	Rate limiting	Important
9	Read replicas	Later
10	Database sharding	Much later
The biggest mindset shift

Don't think:

"How do I make Django handle 50,000 requests/sec?"

Think:

"How do I make sure Django only sees the 1–5% of requests that actually require application logic?"

That's the key.

For a breaking-news website, cacheability is your superpower.

A page like:

/news/iran-conflict-latest


might be requested by 50,000 people, but all 50,000 people can receive essentially the same HTML.

So there is no reason to execute:

Django → PostgreSQL


50,000 times.

You want:

50,000 users
      ↓
CDN
      ↓
~49,500 cache hits

~500 miss
      ↓
Redis

~50 miss
      ↓
Django

~5 miss
      ↓
PostgreSQL


That architecture is far more important than rate limiting or simply adding more Django servers.

And Django's current async support can complement this architecture, particularly for I/O-heavy/concurrent workloads, but caching and eliminating unnecessary work should come first. 
